# TLS terminator for a sovereign RunMyCampus box.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. A box reached at http://10.10.20.137:10000 is not a SECURE
# CONTEXT, so the browser withholds crypto.subtle -- and the offline capability vault
# derives its PIN key with WebCrypto. "Make this device offline ready" could never
# succeed on any browser at that origin. The certificate is the fix; nothing in the
# application layer is.
#
# This file is rendered from the box's own configuration. Regenerate it after any
# change to RMC_EDGE_TLS_MODE / RMC_EDGE_TLS_HOSTNAMES / ALLOWED_HOSTS:
#
#   docker compose -f deploy/selfhost/docker-compose.yml exec web \
#     python manage.py edge_tls --print-caddyfile > deploy/selfhost/Caddyfile.edge
#   docker compose -f deploy/selfhost/docker-compose.yml --profile tls up -d edge-tls
#
# The three HTTPS modes differ by ONE directive, which is the whole reason the
# terminator is Caddy here rather than nginx -- a school moving from self-signed to a
# CA-issued pair changes a line, not a config:
#
#   selfsigned  tls internal                    (Caddy's own local CA)
#   selfsigned  tls /path/tls.crt /path/tls.key (a CA minted by `manage.py edge_tls
#                                                --issue-selfsigned`, when several
#                                                appliances must share one box CA)
#   provided    tls /path/fullchain.pem /path/privkey.pem
#   acme        tls ops@school.example           (public CA, auto-renewing)
#
# The header_up lines are load-bearing. config/settings.py sets
# SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER = ("HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO", "https"); without them every
# request looks like plain HTTP to Django and SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT sends the browser
# into a redirect loop that looks exactly like a broken login.

{
	# A LAN box has no public name to register with a CA, and an admin endpoint that
	# answers on the LAN is a needless surface. Both off by default.
	admin off
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DEFAULT BELOW NAMES NO SCHOOL, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
#
# A Caddy site line is a HOST MATCHER: `gilead.school.lan, 10.10.20.137 {` serves
# that block ONLY for a request whose Host is one of those. A shipped default with
# one school's addresses in it therefore serves every other box nothing at all --
# and nothing at all is what an operator sees, with no error naming the cause.
#
# `:443` matches any host and presents the pair this box minted for all of them, so
# the same file is correct on every box, in every locality, at every address. It is
# also what keeps a box working after its address changes: `edge_tls --ensure`
# reissues the certificate on the next start, and a pinned site line would leave the
# box answering nothing at the address it just healed onto, while every log it wrote
# said healthy.
#
# This widens nothing. ALLOWED_HOSTS in Django is still the Host-header guard.
# Caddy is the terminator here, not the gate.
#
# It requires the certificate to exist first:
#
#   docker compose -f deploy/selfhost/docker-compose.yml exec web \
#     python manage.py edge_tls --issue-selfsigned
#
# Until it does, this container will fail to start and say so. That is deliberate:
# a terminator that refuses to start is a fault you fix in a minute, and a
# terminator that starts and serves an untrusted certificate is a fault a school
# discovers device by device.
#
# A box on a PINNED address with a public name -- an ACME box, or one where the
# named block is wanted for per-host behaviour -- should render its own instead:
#
#   docker compose -f deploy/selfhost/docker-compose.yml exec -T web \
#     python manage.py edge_tls --print-caddyfile > deploy/selfhost/Caddyfile.edge
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:443 {
	tls /app/var/edge-tls/tls.crt /app/var/edge-tls/tls.key
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy web:10000 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
		header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
	}
}

# Someone who types the bare address reaches HTTPS instead of a dead port. 302 and
# never 301: a permanent redirect is cached by the browser and becomes a one-way
# door on this host, the same trap HSTS sets on a LAN.
:80 {
	redir https://{host}{uri} 302
}
